<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/intermediate/class-and-stratification">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Stratification is <strong>how societies sort people into unequal positions — and how the
    sorting persists</strong>. Sociology's contribution is seeing class as structure, not
    individual outcome: the rungs, ladders and escalators exist before anyone climbs.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the definitions, the mobility curve, cultural capital, reproduction" widget="equationStep" config={classCfg} />

  <h2>Findings worth knowing cold</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Absolute vs relative mobility:</strong> absolute (everyone richer than parents — common in growing economies) vs relative (your rank vs your parents' rank — sticky everywhere). Confusing the two produces most bad mobility takes.</li>
    <li><strong>The subjective–objective gap:</strong> most people say they're "middle class" regardless of position — identity lags structure, which is why class politics is unpredictable.</li>
    <li><strong>Precarity is the new margin:</strong> gig work, unstable schedules — the insecurity once concentrated at the bottom has crept upward (Standing's "precariat"), reshaping class experience without abolishing the old categories.</li>
    <li><strong>Intersection with everything else:</strong> class compounds with race, gender and geography (the next two lessons) — single-axis analysis misidentifies both the problem and the policy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Marx; Weber, Economy and Society (1922); Bourdieu, Distinction (1979); Corak on the Gatsby curve; Chetty et al. mobility data.</p>
</TopicPage>
